module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.an_arm_pattern_shape_is_re_derived_from_syntax_instead_of_the_binding

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { AtCeiling, FoundOnRung, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { MechanicallyPreventable, StructurallyGuaranteed }

data an_arm_pattern_shape_is_re_derived_from_syntax_instead_of_the_binding: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "an_arm_pattern_shape_is_re_derived_from_syntax_instead_of_the_binding" as NonEmptyStr,

  // FOUND ON A RUNG, NOT BELOW IT: the class was found by reading the two readers against each other, and
  // the executed leg named below shows the two readings AGREE on main for the shape the corpus actually
  // contains -- so no silent wrongness of this class is on the record. The disagreement is reachable the
  // moment a route records a binding that does not leave the shape on the node, and that route is the one
  // #15671 adds. MECHANICALLY PREVENTABLE is therefore the rung it was found at: one classifier reading
  // one recorded fact removes the pair of derivations, and this PR carries that change. CEILING:
  // structurally guaranteed -- with one authority there is no second derivation left to disagree.
  rung: RungRead {
    found_at: FoundOnRung { rung: MechanicallyPreventable },
    standing: AtCeiling {
      rung: StructurallyGuaranteed,
      ceiling_reason: "one classifier reads one recorded fact: an arm atom is a constructor because the resolved occurrence names a declaration, and infer consumes that record instead of re-deriving the shape from the pattern's syntax. With a single authority there is no pair of readings to diverge, so the misclassification is not refused -- it is unrepresentable."
    }
  },

  receipts: [
    "**an arm pattern's shape is re-derived from syntax instead of taken from the binding** (inference decides whether a match arm is a constructor arm or a catch-all binder by looking at the PATTERN as the front end left it on the node, rather than at what the front end bound that atom to. The two derivations are independent, so they agree only where the front end happens to leave a node shape that mirrors its own binding.)",

    "THE TWO READINGS, ON main AT THE HEAD #15671 BRANCHED FROM. v2.compiler.infer infer_coproduct_arm_row (:3338) classifies each arm through infer_coproduct_arm_pattern (:3175): a node carrying a LEXICAL REFERENCE label is ArmPatternBinder, and that row is minted with `covers: CoversAll, catch_all_binder: true` (:3349-3350); any other shape goes to infer_coproduct_arm_pattern_constructed (:3189), which reads `declaration_reference_path_optional` off the node and makes the tag the last path segment -- so a bare arm atom is a VARIANT arm exactly when resolve left a declaration path on it, and an atom left as a bare atom with no path is ArmPatternUnread (a refusal, not a silent reading). Downstream, infer_coproduct_arm_after_catch_all (:3682) refuses every arm AFTER a CoversAll row as unreachable, and the exhaustiveness fold reads the same `covers` field; gunbc#15380's Rule R (infer_coproduct_catch_all_binder_widening_over_a_nullary, :3623) reads `catch_all_binder`. Three further readers branch on the same classification (:2969, :10522, :10606).",

    "WHAT THE EXECUTED LEG SHOWS, AND THE CLAIM IT RETRACTS. At 4000ff2c5dcd plus ONLY the claim arms (row cc2-mbt-red-b74d, srv2, feature-built seed a6f3b4d6), the fixture `match c { DA => 0  CA => 1  CB => 2 }` -- `type C = CA | CB`, `type D = DA | DB`, `DA` an arm of a DIFFERENT coproduct, visible by name -- refuses with infer_match_pattern_variant_not_declared and NOT with infer_match_arm_unreachable. So on main an arm atom the NAME DOOR resolves is classified as a variant arm, and the claim filed earlier in this PR's review -- that an imported nullary constructor arm is recorded as a catch-all binder on main, a silent-wrongness specimen -- is WRONG and is retracted here. The agreement is nonetheless a coincidence of node shape, not of construction: the classifier never asks what resolve bound, it asks what shape resolve left, and those coincide only for routes that mint a declaration path on the node.",

    "WHERE THE CLASS IS ACTUALLY REACHED. #15671 teaches resolve to bind a bare arm atom to an arm of the EXPECTED TYPE's declared constructor set -- the A2 population, an arm spelling the name door never resolved (`Value =>` under `OperationBehavior`, `Absent =>` under an Optional-shaped domain). That route records the binding in the occurrence index; it does not have to leave a declaration path on the node. Read on its own, the pair then disagrees: resolve records a declaration, infer's shape test reads a binder or an unread pattern, the row is CoversAll, the arms after it are refused as unreachable, and exhaustiveness is discharged by an arm the author wrote as a case. jolly-cat-271 read the pair of readers and reported that disagreement BEFORE the resolve hunk shipped; tidy-ant-251 ruled the infer leg into the PR (option (i): infer classifies from resolve's recorded binding); keen-heron-809 required that the classifier key on WHAT the atom is bound to rather than which route found it, and required the name-door path be folded into the single classifier rather than left beside it. The executed witness of the disagreement is a ref inside this PR's own lineage -- the head with resolve's leg and inference at main's classifier -- not a state main ever had.",

    "THE HARM, IF THE DISAGREEMENT IS ALLOWED IN. A coverage verdict that is not the source's. A module matching a nullary arm first either loses its later arms -- refused as unreachable, with the correction pointing at the arm that was meant as a case -- or gets an exhaustiveness discharge from an arm the author never intended to be a catch-all. Both directions are silent at the point of the error, and the exhaustiveness direction is the dangerous one: a missing case is reported as covered. This row records the class so that a later change to either reader, in either direction, has a named thing to break.",

    "NOT TO BE MERGED WITH a_bare_type_name_binds_the_wrong_declaration, which is about a NAME binding the wrong DECLARATION across modules. Here the binding is correct or absent; the defect would be that a SECOND reader ignores it and re-derives the shape from the node. The two share only the symptom 'the machine and the author disagree', which does not discriminate.",

    "THE REPAIR, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT CLOSE. #15671 replaces the classifier with one that reads the ResolvedTree through resolved_tree_occurrence_resolution: no recorded declaration means today's binder; a recorded declaration -- reached by the name door or by the expected-type door, classified by WHAT it is bound to and not by which route found it -- means a constructor arm, which the existing infer_match_variant_payload membership check then accepts or refuses as a normal variant with no widening to a catch-all. Claimed in v2.test.claim.match_binder.match_binder_typing: mbt_an_arm_the_name_door_cannot_see_binds_by_the_scrutinee_type_and_is_counted_as_that_arm_holds (a two-module fixture, an arm the name door cannot see, asserting the route infers and no arm-shape reason fires) and mbt_an_arm_spelled_as_a_foreign_coproduct_s_arm_refuses_the_site_not_the_next_arm_holds (the name-door route, whose diagnostic main already gets -- a guard that the fold did not move it, not a RED). Residual, stated rather than hidden: an atom with NO recorded declaration and NO visible nullary constructor keeps today's binder reading, and an atom whose spelling names a visible nullary constructor under an unreadable expected type now refuses with resolve_reason_pattern_atom_constructor_type_unresolved instead of being read either way -- the hole is narrowed to 'a spelling that names no constructor in scope', not closed."
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
